Equivalence Checking Equivalence checking underpins applications such as performance optimization (Shypula et al., 2023; Cummins et al., 2023, 2024), code transpilation (Lu et al., 2021; Yang et al., 2024b; Ibrahimzada et al., 2024; Pan et al., 2024), refactoring (Pailoor et al., 2024), and testing (Felsing et al., 2014; Tian et al., 2024). Due to its undecidable nature, no algorithm can decide program equivalence for all program pairs while always terminating. Existing techniques (Sharma et al.，2013;Dahiya and Bansal, 2017; Gupta et al., 2018; Mora et al., 2018; Churchill et al., 2019;Badihi et al., 202O) focus on specific domains， such as SQL query equivalence (Zhao et al., 2023; Ding et al.， 2023； Singh and Bedathur, 2024). EQBENCH (Badihi et al., 2021) and SeqCoBench (Maveli et al., 2024) are the main datasets for equivalence checking, but have limitations. EQBENCH is too small (272 pairs） for LLM evaluation,while SeqCoBench relies only on statement-level syntactic changes (e.g., renaming variables). In contrast, our work introduces a broader set of equivalence categories, creating a more systematic and challenging benchmark.

